call mtr.add_suppression("Setting thread .* nice to 0 failed");
call mtr.add_suppression("Failed to set (I/O thread|purge coordinator thread) priority");
#
# Scenario 1: explicit instance count above any real host's physical
# core count, topology available (no debug point) - the C1 cap fires
# and reduces the instance count to this host's actual physical core
# count (call it N). Since N <= N trivially, the "instances <= cores"
# branch of the thread-count formula applies, and the INIT_COMPLETED
# line should name exactly N parallel threads - both numbers (capped
# instance count and thread count) come from the server's own
# output, cross-checked against each other and against
# @@innodb_buffer_pool_instances.
#
# restart: --innodb-buffer-pool-instances=200 --innodb-buffer-pool-size=1G
include/wait_for_pattern_in_file.inc [Buffer pool initialization completed]
actual_instances read back from the server after requesting 200 instances
PASS: capped instance count and thread count are self-consistent
#
# Scenario 2: topology forced unavailable (debug point), same large
# request as scenario 1 - the C1 cap is skipped here (guard branch),
# so the instance count is honored as requested, but the thread
# count falls back to the literal pre-patch formula
# min(min(sysconf_onln(), 8), n_instances): capped at 8 by that
# formula's own hardcoded ceiling (not a host property - see header
# comment), and distinct from both the (skipped) instance count and
# from silently falling back to a single thread.
#
# restart: --debug=d,bp_topology_unavailable --innodb-buffer-pool-instances=200 --innodb-buffer-pool-size=1G
include/wait_for_pattern_in_file.inc [CPU topology unavailable, buffer pool instance cap skipped]
guard-branch instance count matches the request: 200
include/assert_grep.inc [topology-unavailable diagnostic line is present exactly once]
PASS: guard-branch thread count is within the fallback formula's [1, 8] range and does not silently track the instance count
#
# Scenario 3: INIT_COMPLETED is SYSTEM level - visible at
# log-error-verbosity=1, the strictest (most filtered) setting, same
# reasoning as the CPU/NUMA topology dump uses. Instance count here
# is this host's own derived physical core count (scenario 1) so
# that "instances <= cores" holds without assuming anything about
# the host - not reused from a --debug run, so this checks a plain,
# realistic startup.
#
include/read_file_to_var.inc
reusing this host's derived physical core count for this restart
# restart
PASS: a new INIT_COMPLETED line appeared under log-error-verbosity=1
# restart:
